>>> title: "The Agentic Layer"
>>> lesson_url: "https://agenticengineer.com/tactical-agentic-coding/course/the-agentic-layer"
>>> lesson_subtitle: "Learn the one meta-tactic to rule them all. The single idea you need to become irreplaceable in phase 2 of the Generative AI age."
>>> level: "Advanced"
>>> tactic: {
 "name": "Prioritize Agentics",
 "number": 8,
 "one_liner": "Spend the majority of your engineering time on the agentic layer \u2014 the ring of prompts, template meta prompts, and AI developer workflows wrapped around your codebase \u2014 because every hour invested there teaches compute to do the application-layer work for you.",
 "why_uncomfortable": "It demands you stop doing the very work that feels most productive. Writing application code, fixing the bug yourself, shipping the feature by hand \u2014 all of it is now, by default, the wrong layer. Dan's rule of thumb is that at least half your engineering time (more when bootstrapping) goes to infrastructure that doesn't ship user-visible value directly, on the promise that the return is parabolic: 10 minutes in, two hours out. And it carries a humbling corollary: 'Do not make the mistake of thinking you're smarter than your best engineering practices templated into your agentic layer' \u2014 you must trust the encoded system over your in-the-moment self, because a template meta prompt running in an ADW that kicks off five to ten agents per problem will out-execute you.",
 "compresses_all_tactics": "Dan states this tactic 'has been staring us in the face the entire time' and 'represents all others, from target zero-touch engineering all the way back to stop coding. All tactics are compressed into this tactic.'"
}
>>> guiding_question: {
 "question": "Am I working on the agentic layer or am I working in the application layer?",
 "framing": "Revealed at ~52:39 as 'the final question\u2026 a final meta tactic' and earlier teased as 'the oil of this course' (~2:14) \u2014 all Agentic KPIs, every leverage point, and all eight tactics compress into it. 'Even if you forget all eight tactics, this is all you need to think about.'",
 "right_answer": "The majority of your time should be on the agentic layer, because you can teach compute how to build, teach compute how to engineer your way, and scale compute beyond comprehension \u2014 orchestrating intelligence.",
 "acceptable_reasons_for_application_layer": [
  "A deadline forces direct work",
  "You're testing something by hand",
  "You're running your prompts in the loop specifically to verify they'll work out of the loop"
 ],
 "otherwise": "\"Or else you're moving in the wrong direction. You're going sideways while other engineers\u2026 will be moving up.\""
}
>>> agentic_kpis_recap: {
 "attempts": "Decrease the attempts it takes to deliver work with your agents",
 "size": "Increase the size of work you can hand off",
 "streak": "Increase the streak of successful work shipped without mistakes",
 "presence": "Drop your presence \u2014 showing up to prompt, review, babysit \u2014 to the bare minimum, target zero (stay out the loop)"
}
>>> estimated_print_pages_when_expanded: 52
>>> current_practice_gaps: [
 {
  "gap": "Slash-command prompt libraries \u2192 Agent Skills",
  "why": "tac-8 encodes every reusable behavior as .claude/commands/*.md invoked by name from ADWs. Current Claude Code packages reusable expertise as Agent Skills (SKILL.md with frontmatter, trigger descriptions, on-demand loading, shareable across repos/teams) \u2014 the modern answer to 'template your engineering.' The lesson's meta-prompt concept maps 1:1; the packaging and discovery upgraded.",
  "yt_candidates": [
   "X2ciJedw2vU"
  ]
 },
 {
  "gap": "Hand-rolled subprocess agent.py \u2192 Claude Agent SDK",
  "why": "Every app copies a ~630-line agent.py that shells out to `claude -p --output-format stream-json` and hand-parses JSONL. Dan already plants the successor on camera (agent_sdk.py, 'they support both a Python version and a bun version'). That SDK matured into the Claude Agent SDK (Python/TS): typed messages, sessions, hooks, subagents, structured outputs \u2014 production ADWs today start there, while the subprocess form remains the clearest teaching form.",
  "yt_candidates": []
 },
 {
  "gap": "--dangerously-skip-permissions everywhere \u2192 permission modes, sandboxing, hooks",
  "why": "All tac-8 ADWs run agents with permissions fully bypassed (fine for isolated demo repos, risky as a habit). Current practice gates headless agents with allowlists, permission modes, sandboxed bash, and pre/post tool-use hooks \u2014 and worktree isolation (which the lesson DOES teach) is now complemented by first-class sandboxing. The lesson's isolation instinct is right; the bypass flag is the dated part.",
  "yt_candidates": []
 },
 {
  "gap": "DIY cron/polling triggers \u2192 native background agents, scheduled runs, CI hooks",
  "why": "app2/app4 poll with `schedule` loops in long-running terminal processes; app5 hand-rolls a FastAPI server. The trigger CONCEPT is timeless, but current tooling offers headless CI invocation, background tasks, scheduled cloud agents, and webhook-native integrations out of the box \u2014 a modern reader should map 'trigger' to those services rather than assume a hand-rolled poller is required.",
  "yt_candidates": []
 },
 {
  "gap": "One serial agent per task \u2192 first-class parallel subagents and orchestration",
  "why": "tac-8 achieves parallelism at the OS level: detached subprocesses per task, one Claude instance each, coordinated by markdown/Notion state. Current tools offer in-band multi-agent primitives (subagents, parallel tool fan-out, worktree-isolated background agents, orchestration frameworks), so the lesson's parallel patterns can now be expressed inside the agent runtime itself \u2014 same architecture, less scaffolding.",
  "yt_candidates": [
   "fop_yxV-mPo"
  ]
 },
 {
  "gap": "Model/tool period detail",
  "why": "Scripts toggle sonnet|opus of the course era; the app5 start script hard-codes Dan's local claude path; the Notion MCP server and .mcp.json format are course-era versions. All incidental: swap in current model names/paths and everything runs. Dan future-proofs this explicitly ('tools will change and models will improve \u2014 we can't fixate').",
  "yt_candidates": []
 },
 {
  "gap": "AEA foreshadows in-product agents",
  "why": "app5's agent-embedded-application (SQLite session store + resume + in-app chat windows) is a hand-built preview of what the Agent SDK now supports directly: embedding long-lived, session-resuming agents into products. The 'outside the loop is inside the application' idea has gone from demo to standard architecture.",
  "yt_candidates": []
 }
]
>>> yt_overlay_candidates: [
 {
  "video_id": "fop_yxV-mPo",
  "title": "Codebase Singularity",
  "maps_to": "operationalizes tactic",
  "include_in_phase1": true,
  "rationale": "The free-channel treatment of exactly this lesson's end-state: the codebase that increasingly runs itself as the agentic layer thickens. Was explicitly deferred from the TAC-1 chapter ('reserve for a later chapter') \u2014 TAC-8 is that chapter; zero topic drift from 'prioritize your agentic layer.'"
 },
 {
  "video_id": "hGg3nWp7afg",
  "title": "3 Codebase Folders",
  "maps_to": "named hole",
  "include_in_phase1": true,
  "rationale": "Directly expands the minimum-viable-agentic-layer directory story (prompts/plans/ai_docs-style folders that serve agents) \u2014 the lesson names the MVAL pieces but moves fast; this overlay slows down on the folder-level reasoning. Also deferred from TAC-1 to the context/structure lesson family."
 },
 {
  "video_id": "X2ciJedw2vU",
  "title": "RAW Agentic Coding: ZERO to Agent SKILL",
  "maps_to": "corrects outdated",
  "include_in_phase1": false,
  "rationale": "Upgrades .claude/commands prompt-library thinking to Agent Skills packaging \u2014 the lesson's biggest current-practice gap \u2014 but it already anchors the TAC-1 chapter's overlay set; listed here for the volume map, not for double placement."
 }
]
>>> prerequisites_tools: [
 "TAC lessons 1\u20137 (this lesson recaps and compresses all prior tactics, KPIs, and leverage points)",
 "Claude Code with API funds or a plan (ADWs run with --dangerously-skip-permissions in programmatic mode; ANTHROPIC_API_KEY via .env)",
 "Python + UV (all ADWs are uv single-file scripts with inline dependency blocks)",
 "Bun (app1 /start, app3 server/client, app4 bun prototypes) and Node.js 18+",
 "Git with worktree support (app2/app4/app5 isolation) and the GitHub CLI for app5's issue-driven ADWs",
 "Optional per app: Notion account + @notionhq/notion-mcp-server via .mcp.json and NOTION_AGENTIC_TASK_TABLE_ID (app4); OpenAI/Anthropic keys for the NLQ-to-SQL app; ElevenLabs/OpenAI keys for app3 TTS hooks",
 "An IDE (Dan uses VS Code windows per worktree: `code trees/<name>`)"
]

