content.content:
>>> quotes: [
 {
  "text": "Imagine a ring around your codebase. At first it's thin, then it becomes thicker as you scale agentics into your codebase. Machines that operate with your judgment, shipping your way, getting work done autonomously in your product.",
  "approx_timestamp": "1:05"
 },
 {
  "text": "This guiding question represents the oil of this course. All the Agentic KPIs, every tactic, it compresses all of it into a single question.",
  "approx_timestamp": "2:14"
 },
 {
  "text": "Prioritize agentics. This tactic has been staring us in the face the entire time.",
  "approx_timestamp": "3:01"
 },
 {
  "text": "As a good starting point, I recommend half of your engineering time, at least half, should be spent on the new agentic layer.",
  "approx_timestamp": "4:00"
 },
 {
  "text": "Keep your eyes on the primitives\u2026 It's the primitives, right? Claude Code represents the first viable engineering tool that unlocks agentic engineering\u2026 but it won't be the last.",
  "approx_timestamp": "4:50"
 },
 {
  "text": "The only thing that matters is that you can compose code and reference other files and have this be isolated from your application layer.",
  "approx_timestamp": "7:35"
 },
 {
  "text": "These are meta prompts. They template out what your engineering workflows look like\u2026 This is a prompt that generates a prompt.",
  "approx_timestamp": "9:49"
 },
 {
  "text": "This script is the gateway into agentic coding\u2026 This ADW is calling an agent. This is distinctly different from any other type of code.",
  "approx_timestamp": "13:39"
 },
 {
  "text": "By creating a script that can surround the application, that can surround any unit of code, we are slowly moving out the loop.",
  "approx_timestamp": "15:01"
 },
 {
  "text": "It's a great place to start. It's a great place to do deep work\u2026 It's a terrible place to stay, okay? So many engineers are going to miss this mark.",
  "approx_timestamp": "15:20"
 },
 {
  "text": "Building an agentic layer is parabolic. It's absolutely parabolic. Put 10 minutes in, get two hours of value out or more.",
  "approx_timestamp": "15:38"
 },
 {
  "text": "Remember: prioritize agentics. Prioritize the agentic layer of your codebase. Don't do the work by yourself. Teach your agents to do it. Teach them how to solve the problem class. Build the system that builds the system.",
  "approx_timestamp": "26:26"
 },
 {
  "text": "You will get to a point where your ADWs, in combination with your prompts, is its own kind of micro codebase \u2014 a wrangle layer around your codebase.",
  "approx_timestamp": "27:45"
 },
 {
  "text": "I have solved the problem class of building Astral UV model context protocol servers. I've solved it. It's done.",
  "approx_timestamp": "38:50"
 },
 {
  "text": "The power of agents isn't in one, it's in many.",
  "approx_timestamp": "40:38"
 },
 {
  "text": "One agent, one prompt, one purpose\u2026 This creates focused agents, focused context windows, that are reproducible and easily testable, validatable, and improvable.",
  "approx_timestamp": "47:53"
 },
 {
  "text": "You realize you're the bottleneck and then you'll convert the out-loop framework from PITER to PITE\u2026 You'll drop the R off\u2026 You will let your product ship itself.",
  "approx_timestamp": "49:21"
 },
 {
  "text": "Don't make the mistake of thinking that you can outperform your team's best engineering practices encoded into a template metaprompt that runs in an AI developer workflow that kicks off five to ten agents per problem.",
  "approx_timestamp": "51:30"
 },
 {
  "text": "Am I working on the agentic layer or am I working in the application layer? That's it. On a day-to-day tactical level, even if you forget all eight tactics, this is all you need to think about.",
  "approx_timestamp": "52:39"
 },
 {
  "text": "There is no competing with an agentic engineer. You must have fleets of agents operating on your behalf to compete with an agentic engineer.",
  "approx_timestamp": "53:40"
 },
 {
  "text": "An agentic engineer operates on living pieces of software that work while they sleep.",
  "approx_timestamp": "55:34"
 },
 {
  "text": "What we're seeing here\u2026 is the commoditization of implementation and early signs of the commoditization of engineering.",
  "approx_timestamp": "56:53"
 },
 {
  "text": "This is bigger than a course. This is a manifesto for the next phase of software engineering\u2026 the codex for agentic engineering.",
  "approx_timestamp": "57:45"
 },
 {
  "text": "When the agents arrived, something changed. We weren't just AI coding anymore. We started orchestrating intelligence.",
  "approx_timestamp": "1:02:20"
 }
]
>>> loot_box_mentions: [
 "Lesson 8 ships 12 loot notes (captured in course-notes.md): The Agentic Layer Ring; The Single Guiding Question; Tactic #8 Prioritize Agentics; 50%+ Time on Agentic Layer; Agentic Layer vs Application Layer; the tac-8 repo clone; Focus on the Primitives; Minimum Viable Agentic Layer; Problem Classes Not One-Offs; The Gateway Script; Moving Out of the Loop; Parabolic Value of the Agentic Layer.",
 "Codebase: git clone https://github.com/disler/tac-8.git",
 "In-lesson pointer at ~39:29: 'check your loot box, all this is going to be available to you \u2014 don't fixate on the exact details, focus on the agentic layer.'"
]
>>> common_weak_student_failures: [
 "Nodding at the ratio, then spending 100% of the week in the application layer 'just to hit the deadline' \u2014 never bootstrapping the layer that would have hit the next ten deadlines. The tactic is a time-allocation change, not a belief.",
 "Cloning tac-8 and reading the code instead of RUNNING it: the lesson's value is watching a trigger pick up work you didn't dispatch by hand ('I'm not doing this by the way').",
 "Building prompts but no ADW directory \u2014 staying a prompt collector. If you're only operating with prompts, you're missing out on tons of capabilities; the fusion of deterministic code with prompts IS the layer.",
 "Copy-pasting Dan's directory shapes as gospel. He says the opposite three times: 'this is just an arbitrary agentic layer\u2026 you can build it any way you want' \u2014 the invariants are isolation, composability, observability, feedback loops.",
 "Mixing the layers: agent scripts inside apps/, or agents writing directly to main without worktrees \u2014 then concluding multi-agent 'doesn't work' when parallel edits collide.",
 "Skipping the tags/blocking semantics in tasks.md and writing one giant task \u2014 never seeing dependencies (\u23f0), model upgrades ({opus}), or workflow routing ({adw_plan_implement_update_task}) that make the board a real orchestrator.",
 "Treating one-agent-one-prompt-one-purpose as optional and building a sprawling conversational mega-agent \u2014 losing reproducibility and testability, the exact failure the rule prevents (context windows are still small).",
 "Dropping review before adding feedback loops \u2014 PITE without validation commands is not zero-touch engineering, it's unsupervised risk. The R is REPLACED by encoded review, never merely deleted.",
 "Fixating on the exact tools (Claude Code flags, Notion, VS Code) instead of the primitives \u2014 the lesson explicitly de-emphasizes Claude Code, and 'don't fixate on the exact details, focus on the agentic layer.'",
 "Answering the guiding question honestly but only once. It's a DAILY question \u2014 'every day when you sit down to work' \u2014 not a course-completion checkbox."
]

